AB  - Significant financial losses and a decline in confidence in sectors including banking, healthcare, and self-driving cars are the results of serious issues brought on by software defects. RT-CPFP, a novel framework for real-time defect prediction in various applications, is presented in this paper. It integrates automated machine learning, internet learning, and transfer learning. By addressing the problems of limited data and shifting topic areas, TL facilitates knowledge sharing between source and target activities. OL lessens the chance of concept drift by regulating frequent code changes and updating the model in little steps. By automatically choosing features, models, and settings, AutoML simplifies the process and improves overall efficiency. Using the NASA and PROMISE datasets, RT-CPFP yielded an F1-score of 0. With an area under the curve (AUC) of 0, the score is 86. It outperforms the benchmarks by 812% with a score of 91 and a latency of less than 100 milliseconds. Working with continuous integration and continuous deployment (CI/CD) systems is made simple by the framework's architecture, which is depicted in a straightforward picture. Among the contributions are a robust and adaptable architecture, extensive testing using real-world data, and practical DevOps deployment strategies. Distribution inconsistencies, codebase modifications, and human adjustments are all handled by RTCPFP. In today's software development, it offers a fresh approach to address issues before they arise. Agile processes may make extensive use of this strategy.
ER  - 

