AB  - Infrastructure as Code (IaC) has emerged as a cornerstone of modern cloud-native application management, yet ensuring state consistency and concurrency safety remains a persistent challenge. Conventional IaC tools such as Terraform and Ansible rely on coarse-grained locking mechanisms that prevent concurrent state updates but often introduce bottlenecks, idle waiting, and reduced parallelism. This work explores the use of Machine Learning (ML) to enhance IaC concurrency control by predicting potential conflicts, estimating optimal lock durations, and dynamically adjusting locking granularity. We generated a large-scale dataset of 124,000 state-modification events and evaluated both Random Forest (RF) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) models using stratified group k-fold cross-validation. Results show that RF achieves strong baseline performance while the LSTM consistently outperforms tree-based baselines in capturing sequential conflict patterns. These improvements translate to more accurate detection of high-risk updates and lower false positives, enabling finer-grained concurrency without sacrificing safety. We further analyze model overhead in deployment contexts and highlight design trade-offs for explainability versus predictive accuracy. By releasing the dataset, models, and training pipeline, this work provides a reproducible foundation for ML-driven IaC concurrency management and opens new directions for adaptive, conflict-aware DevOps automation.
ER  - 

