AB  - The growing importance of DevOps, DataOps, MLOps, and AIOps in software development demands new approaches in software engineering education. This study introduces a challenge-based instructional intervention designed to foster operational thinking among students. The intervention combined real-world scenarios, visual artifacts, and peer collaboration to support engagement and applied learning.A mixed-methods evaluation was conducted using Likert-scale surveys, open-ended feedback, observational data, and peer-assessed group outputs. Results indicate high levels of student participation, meaningful inter-group collaboration, and improved confidence in applying X-Ops principles. Students appreciated the exploratory learning environment, though some expressed a need for clearer initial guidance.The study highlights the potential of active, visually supported methodologies to prepare students for the operational demands of modern software systems. It also provides a foundation for future research into scalable, practice-oriented instructional models in computing education.
