AB  - Distributed enterprise platforms increasingly rely on microservices, containerized workloads, service meshes, event-driven pipelines, edge components, and hybrid cloud infrastructure. Although these architectures improve scalability and deployment agility, they also increase telemetry fragmentation, alert overload, incident localization delays, and coordination gaps between reliability engineering and security operations. This study investigates how AI-augmented runtime observability enhances distributed platform resilience through intelligent telemetry correlation, adaptive anomaly detection, incident localization efficiency, and runtime security orchestration. Drawing on Dynamic Capabilities Theory and Socio-Technical Systems Theory, the study conceptualizes AI-augmented observability as a runtime sensing capability, telemetry correlation and anomaly detection as analytical capabilities, and incident localization and security orchestration as operational response capabilities. Survey data were collected from 412 DevOps engineers, site reliability engineers, cloud architects, cybersecurity analysts, platform engineers, and IT infrastructure managers. The proposed model was tested using partial least squares structural equation modelling. The results show that AI-augmented runtime observability has a strong positive effect on intelligent telemetry correlation ($\beta$ = 0.684, p < 0.001), which in turn significantly enhances adaptive anomaly detection ($\beta$ = 0.617, p < 0.001). Adaptive anomaly detection significantly improves both incident localization efficiency ($\beta$ = 0.593, p < 0.001) and runtime security orchestration ($\beta$ = 0.642, p < 0.001). Incident localization efficiency ($\beta$ = 0.376, p < 0.001) and runtime security orchestration ($\beta$ = 0.421, p < 0.001) further contribute significantly to distributed platform resilience. Mediation analysis confirms that intelligent telemetry correlation, adaptive anomaly detection, incident localization efficiency, and runtime security orchestration transmit the effect of AI-augmented observability on resilience outcomes. The study contributes to AIOps, DevSecOps, cloud-native security, and platform resilience research by empirically validating an integrated operational intelligence model that links observability, anomaly detection, incident response, and runtime security orchestration in distributed enterprise environments.
ER  - 

