AB  - Machine Learning Operations (MLOps) extends Development and Operations (DevOps) practices to manage the end-to-end lifecycle of machine learning models. However, the alignment between MLOps and the traditional software development lifecycle (SDLC) remains insufficiently defined. This study presents a systematic literature review of peer-reviewed works published between 2018 and 2025 in IEEE Xplore, Scopus, and the ACM Digital Library. The review was conducted following a protocol for evidence-based software engineering. A total of 71 primary studies were included after deduplication and screening. A phase-based taxonomy of MLOps tools was synthesized and an SDLC-MLOps compatibility framework was derived to classify alignment levels as high, medium, or low across development phases. Inter-rater agreement, measured using Cohen's Kappa, yielded a value of 0.91, while the average study quality score was 0.73. The results show convergence around pipelines, model registries, and containerized deployment, alongside persistent fragmentation in process models and organizational practices. The findings provide implications for practice, including maturity-based adoption strategies, role definition, governance mechanisms, and continuous monitoring. Additionally, opportunities for future research are identified in the areas of ML-specific testing and harmonized metrics across software engineering and data science disciplines.
ER  - 

