AB  - Java Virtual Machine (JVM) applications are critical to enterprise solutions, and for good reason: they are resilient, portable, and scalable. However, performance bottlenecks still exist and one of the most important reasons is due to memory leaks and inefficient caching. Traditional profiling tools like VisualVM, JConsole etc are reactive, meaning many times the intervention is manual and adjustments to dynamic runtime requirement are not very flexible. This work presents novel, machine learning based approach to detect JVM memory leaks and optimize the cache intelligently in real-time. It uses telemetry data such as heap usage, frequency of garbage collection, object allocation rates, and performance measures for caches. On a synthetic dataset of a JVM with simulated known anomalies, we evaluated four models—Autoencoder, Isolation Forest, One-Class SVM, and Random Forest. ROC-AUC score value of 0.9820 and 0.9868 were achieved by the Autoencoder among the Random Forest model, which shown excellent classification performance. The Autoencoder demonstrated useful in unsupervised scenarios, effectively pinpointing subtle behavioral anomalies in the use of memory. The framework provides a fine-grained anomaly identification and serves as a starting point of reinforcement learning based automatic cache leakage elimination. Our results highlight the disruptive potential of machine learning in diagnosing JVM-level problems, reducing downtime, and improving application performance. The approach is scalable, flexible and and suitable to integrate into DevOps observability for intelligent Java applications oversight. The code is available on the GitHub repository https://github.com/Srikanthrgudi/Diagnosing-Java-Memory-Leaks-and-Performance-
ER  - 

