AB  - Organizations that employ Agile and DevOps methodologies are experiencing increasing pertinent entrepreneurial cybersecurity threats and risks and challenges. Rapid-release cycles have been reported to have an incident rate of 68 % among enterprises. This dissertation describes an initial, pathbreaking, predictive, 'Realtime Cyber Risk Score' AI model that assesses real-time cyber risks in CI/CD pipelines. This model applies machine learning (Random Forest, XGBoost, and Neural Networks) and static and dynamic code analysis, dependency vulnerability scanning, and behavioral analysis of 2,847 deployments from 12 company environments over 18 months. This model predicts, with 94.7 % (4.2 % false positive) risk accuracy, 89.7 % critical vulnerability precision, and 89.7 % accurate detection of high risks, 89.7 % high risk deployments' production (pre-releases) and general production (pre-releases) crisis deployments. It also led to a 73% reduction (8.4 to 2.3 days) of mean time to vulnerability detection, as well as 156 prevented (through emergency patch implementations) Breach incidents in production testing Application test environments. The 47 risk parameters included for score assessments (complexity and developer behavior metrics) are: Social isolation, configuration drift, third-party dependency trust, and behavioral score of isolation. This significantly leads to an improvement of 31 % over the mean $(28 \%$ reduction of production rollbacks of CI/CD pipelines). The adaptability of the model provides the ability to learn from experience while maintaining 91.8% across several different technology stacks such as Python, Java, Node.js, and. NET. These results provide further confirmation that within a developing environment, the predictive security driven from an AI model can provide maintaining robust security as a developing environment, and can provide organizations with the ability to maintain a balance of speed with risk.
ER  - 

